# SYNOPSIS

```c
vx_status vx_sched_ctx_create(const vx_sched_params *p, vx_handle *out);
vx_status vx_sched_ctx_bind(vx_handle ctx, vx_handle thread, int32_t core);
vx_status vx_sched_ctx_configure(vx_handle ctx, const vx_sched_params *p);
vx_status vx_sched_reserve(vx_handle ctx, uint32_t count, uint32_t cls,
                           uint32_t domain, uint32_t flags, vx_core_set *out);
vx_status vx_intent_set(uint32_t intent);
```

# DESCRIPTION

A thread says what it needs, an intent, rather than a priority or a core (01 §8, ADR-0038, M6 step 6d6c).
The intents are bands of the scheduler, highest first: `VX_INTENT_REALTIME`, `VX_INTENT_INTERACTIVE_FRAME`, `VX_INTENT_INTERACTIVE` (every thread's to start with), `VX_INTENT_THROUGHPUT` and `VX_INTENT_BACKGROUND`.
Threads of one band share the CPUs round robin, in 10 ms slices; a thread made ready in a band above one running preempts it.
A channel message's `sender_intent` is its sender's.

: `sched_ctx_create`
  Makes a scheduling context with the parameters in a `vx_sched_params`, and gives a handle to it with `WRITE`, `MANAGE`, `INSPECT`, `DUPLICATE` and `TRANSFER`.
  A `realtime` context has a `period`, 1 ms to 10 s, and a `budget` of CPU time in each, 100 µs up to the period; it is admitted, or refused with `REFUSED`, never degraded: the budgets of every admitted context together, as fractions of their periods, may come to at most 80% of the CPUs.
  Other intents take neither.
  A context goes with its last handle and its last bound thread, and its budget and reserved CPUs with it.
: `sched_ctx_bind`
  Binds a thread, by a handle with `MANAGE` or `VX_HANDLE_NONE` for the caller, to a context held with `WRITE`; the thread then runs with its intent and, if it is `realtime`, against its budget, which every thread bound to it shares.
  Spent, its threads wait until the next period fills it.
  <core> is -1, or a CPU of the context's reservation, which the thread then runs on, alone.
  A <ctx> of `VX_HANDLE_NONE` unbinds the thread.
: `sched_ctx_configure`
  Changes a context, held with `MANAGE`; a `realtime` one is admitted again, and keeps its old budget if the new one is refused.
  With `VX_HANDLE_NONE` it sets the calling thread's own intent, which may be anything but `realtime`: `vx_intent_set`.
: `sched_reserve`
  Reserves <count> whole CPUs for a context held with `MANAGE`, all of them or `REFUSED`, and gives the grant in a `vx_core_set`, a mask of CPU indices and their count; with a <count> of 0 it gives back what the context has.
  The first CPU is never reserved, so one always stays shared.
  A reserved CPU runs only the context's threads bound to it; what ran there leaves at its next switch.
  <cls> is `VX_CORE_ANY` or `VX_CORE_TIER(0)`, <domain> `VX_DOMAIN_ANY`, and <flags> may name `VX_RESERVE_NO_SMT_SIBLINGS` and `VX_RESERVE_SAME_LLC`.

A thread's scheduling is readable by `thread_state`'s `VX_STATE_GET_SCHED`, a `vx_sched_info`, and as text in procfs(4)'s `/proc/N/threads/T/sched`.

# EXAMPLES

A thread that needs 3 ms of CPU in each 10 ms:

```c
vx_handle rt;
vx_sched_params p = {.intent = VX_INTENT_REALTIME,
                     .period = 10'000'000, .budget = 3'000'000};
if (vx_sched_ctx_create(&p, &rt) == VX_OK)
  vx_sched_ctx_bind(rt, VX_HANDLE_NONE, -1);
```

